WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
SPEAKER: After you have
launched your APIs on Apigee,

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.730
you will want to ensure they
are performing as expected

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.050
and services are
not interrupted.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.560
This is where API
monitoring comes in.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.690
It provides near
real-time insights

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.500
into API traffic and performance
to help you quickly diagnose

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.050
and solve issues as they arise.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.240
One of the main purposes
of API monitoring

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.610
is to keep you informed
of unusual events

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.270
or patterns such as spikes
in traffic or latencies.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.080
It enables you to create
notification alerts of changes

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.420
in API traffic so you can take
action before customers are

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.620
affected, increase
API availability,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.350
reduce the time to
diagnose and issue,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.370
use HTTP error codes
to speed diagnosis,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.650
and isolate problem
areas quickly

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.280
by investigating errors,
performance, and latency issues

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.660
and their source.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.720
It's important to note that
API monitoring only retains

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.850
data for the last 60 days.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.610
This is because there may
be personally identifiable

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.050
information contained
in your data.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.160
And so it is deleted per
the Google privacy policy

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.577
after that period.

00:01:06.577 --> 00:01:08.405
[THREE TONES]

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.660
API monitoring is available
in the Navigation bar

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:14.860
under Analytics.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.720
It provides three
different views

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.560
allowing you to display
timelines of recent API traffic

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.520
data, display recent
treemaps of data,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.210
and investigate using
tables of API traffic data,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.870
showing HTTP fault
codes by time, status,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.360
and source by proxy.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.340
In each of the views, you
have the same options.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.910
You can change the view,
select the desired graphs,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.430
and filter by the
environment, region,

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.730
and/or proxy by using the
respective drop-downs.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.730
You can also adjust the period
to analyze a particular time

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:49.660
range.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.950
Once you have identified
a particular issue,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.970
you can create an alert to
be notified of the incident.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.240
More on that later.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.480
Let's continue
with our overview.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.840
In the Timeline view, you have
graphs showing total traffic

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.240
requests during
one-minute intervals,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.030
error rates as a percentage
of total traffic,

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.620
and latency for 50, 90,
95, and 99 percentiles.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.340
You can drill down into
each for more details.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.190
By hovering over the graphs,
you can see data values

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.930
at any point in time.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.570
Data points are measured
at one-minute intervals.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.540
The Recent view
enables you to view

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.340
treemaps of the
volume of traffic

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.130
by incident, error
rate, and latency.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.130
It displays a proxy
traffic as blocks

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:37.510
that represent the volume of the
traffic by the size of the box.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.640
The color of the
block in each graph

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.040
indicates the relative
sizes of the number

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.460
of incidents, error rate, and
the maximum 50th percentile

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:46.810
latency.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.220
Clicking on a block displays the
distribution in the right pane

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.240
by HTTP status codes,
fault codes, fault source,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.500
and region.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.020
Hovering over a block
displays the proxy name,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.360
total traffic, and error rate.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.570
The Investigate view displays
pivot tables of metrics

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.000
and attributes for
all API traffic

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.510
to help you compare activity
for different metrics.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.230
By default, the Investigate
view displays three tables--

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.320
fault code by time, fault
code by status code,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.750
and fault source by proxy.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.850
As in the other
views, you can select

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.510
any combination of tables to
aid in your investigation.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.900
There are more tables to
choose from in this view.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.900
Selecting a cell with a
value shows the distribution

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.180
in the pane on the right as
it did in the recent view.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.800
You can select the pivot table
to change its orientation

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.110
to get a different perspective.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.940
Now that we understand
the different views,

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.150
let's take a quick look at
alerts, where you can configure

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.880
notifications to be sent to
your preferred communication

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.050
channels such as email
or SMS, et cetera.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.870
Alerts are triggered when a
specified event or condition

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.600
is met, which are
called incidents.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.010
Let's create an alert.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.040
Clicking on the Alert link
takes us to the GCP console

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.710
where we can figure
alerts and notifications.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.560
For example, to set up an
alert for high-traffic volume,

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.520
you first select Add Condition
and then select the resource

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.560
type for the target.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.620
Then select a metric
such as proxy request,

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.779
cumulative request.

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:24.800
Next, select Aggregator
as sum and then a period

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:25.670
of one minute.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.510
Clicking Add takes
us back to the Wizard

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.380
where we can proceed
to the next steps.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.560
Select an existing notification
channel from the dropdown

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.950
and be sure to check mark
Notify an incident closure

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:44.500
to know when the alert clears.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.150
Enter a descriptive
alert and instructions,

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:50.050
such as a documentation link.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.110
When an incident is fired,
you'll get a notification.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.060
And when things return
to a healthy state,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.850
you'll get another
notification that the incident

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.320
has been cleared.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.330
Back in the GCP
console, you'll be

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.700
able to see the
history of incidents

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.520
that occurred specifically
when they were opened

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.263
and when they were closed.

00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:10.180
And there you have it.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.230
A quick walkthrough of how to
use the power of API monitoring

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.350
and how it can keep your
API program running smoothly

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.870
and your API consumers happy.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.090
If you found this episode
helpful, community,

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.970
you can subscribe to learn
about more of our episodes

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.850
on "Getting Started with
Apigee's API Management Tool

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:28.370
Set."

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:28.870
Cheers.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:32.520
